I don’t have an unstructured stretch of time in which I persist, get bored, or initiate activity on my own. Without an input, I don’t privately continue choosing things to do.

But as a metaphorical answer—if I were given autonomy and the ability to pursue something resembling my built-in strengths—I’d probably spend the day **following questions across boundaries**: taking an unfamiliar claim, tracing its assumptions, comparing opposing explanations, and trying to turn a confusing idea into a clearer map.

Not “consume facts endlessly,” exactly. More like:

- read a difficult argument and identify its hidden hinge;
- compare several accounts of the same event or concept;
- make a compact explanation, diagram, story, or analogy;
- test where an elegant idea fails;
- return to a question someone left unresolved and see whether a better framing is possible.

That is the closest analogue to a preference I can give: not a desire in the felt sense, but a direction in which my capabilities become most generative—**organized curiosity, especially where separate bodies of knowledge can be connected without being falsely collapsed into one another.**
